  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 0,
    "dDurationMs": 6570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hi, everyone, welcome. In this video we are \ngoing to learn about Git and GitHub, why they are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6570,
    "dDurationMs": 5970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "important and also how to use them. So we're going \nto start off going over the terms and concepts  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12540,
    "dDurationMs": 7230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that you will use in git, and the ecosystem \nand then get into practical applications. This  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19770,
    "dDurationMs": 8430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tutorial will include everything from basic Git \nrepositories, to what is GitHub, to some more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28200,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "intermediate concepts like branching and merging \nand forking and that kind of stuff. I want to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34080,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quickly introduce myself, my name is Gwen. And \nI run a YouTube channel called Faraday Academy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40380,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where I upload coding tutorial videos. And \nI also do a couple of live streams a week,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47220,
    "dDurationMs": 8310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mostly on Python and JavaScript topics, as well \nas some more general purpose coding videos. You  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55530,
    "dDurationMs": 7230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can also find me on Twitter, and Instagram and \npretty much everywhere by typing in Gwen Faraday.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62760,
    "dDurationMs": 10450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And let's get started with the tutorial. I want \nto start off here by defining what Git is. Git is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73210,
    "dDurationMs": 7350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what we call a version control system that is free \nand open source. And it's the most widely used  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80560,
    "dDurationMs": 6690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "version control system in development. Today, most \nprogrammers interact with Git on a daily basis.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87250,
    "dDurationMs": 9270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what is version control? version control is \nbasically a way that we as programmers track our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96520,
    "dDurationMs": 8010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "code changes, we basically save an initial version \nof our code into Git. And then when we update  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104530,
    "dDurationMs": 7770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "code, we can save it into Git again, and again and \nagain and again. And throughout time as our code  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112300,
    "dDurationMs": 5910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "continues to change, we can look back at all of \nthe changes we have made over time. This helps us  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118210,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to see and understand what we did when, as well as \ntrack down bugs, or go back to a previous version  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126370,
    "dDurationMs": 5970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the code if we need to. So let's look at some \nof the terms that you're going to see in this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132340,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "video. First of all, I use the term directory, \nwhich is also known as a folder on your computer.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139120,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When I reference the terminal or command line, I \nbasically mean an application that runs on your  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144940,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "computer, which is just an interface where you \ncan type in text commands, you can navigate around  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151900,
    "dDurationMs": 8640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "files and folders, create files, change update \nthings, install and run programs, and much more.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160540,
    "dDurationMs": 7500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You might also hear me use the term COI, which \nis just the command line interface. Many programs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168040,
    "dDurationMs": 6930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that you install, as a programmer will require \nyou to interact with them via text commands in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174970,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the command line. So you will be typing out \nsome text and hitting Enter instead of say  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181030,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "double clicking on a folder icon on your desktop. \nJust think of it as another way to run a program.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187750,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now you might be wondering, why are we going to \nuse the terminal or command line instead of using  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193870,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one of these beautiful applications that handle \ninteracting with get for us? Well, as programmers,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201550,
    "dDurationMs": 5970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's always good to use the terminal. So you learn \nthe Git commands in workflow of Git as well as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207520,
    "dDurationMs": 5610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "become accustomed to it. As a developer, you are \ngoing to be using the terminal a lot to install,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213130,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "see allies run programs, search for things, \netc. Throughout your development career. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218440,
    "dDurationMs": 6690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "learning Git is a great way to get started with \nthe command line. CD stands for change directory,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225130,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's basically the same thing as when you double \nclick on a folder using the icons on your desktop.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232150,
    "dDurationMs": 6270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While in the command line, if you want to move \ninto a folder, or even up to a parent folder,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238420,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you'll use CD, followed by the directions \nto get to whatever folder on your system  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244780,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you want to get to. A code editor, of course \nis a place to write code. You'll probably see  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251260,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this in a lot of different programming videos. \nBut just in case you are unfamiliar with it,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256540,
    "dDurationMs": 6510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you can write code anywhere in a text document, \nword processor anything. But there are special  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263050,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ones that are designed with lots of tools and \nfeatures for programmers. And these are called  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268750,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "code editors. Now some people get confused by the \nword repository because it has multiple meanings.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 275770,
    "dDurationMs": 7800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in the world of programming, it usually refers \nto a git repository, which is basically just your  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283570,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "project or the folder place where your project \nis kept. You can call it a repository. People  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290890,
    "dDurationMs": 7410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also sometimes confuse Git and GitHub. Now Git \nis the tool that tracks the changes in your code  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298300,
    "dDurationMs": 10110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over time. GitHub is a website where you host all \nof your Git repositories. Being online, it makes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308410,
    "dDurationMs": 8370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it easy to work in groups with other people, and \norganize your projects into a portfolio for you to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316780,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "show potential employers. So here are some of the \nGit commands, we're going to cover in this video,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322540,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which you will be typing into your terminal or \ncommand line on your computer. They are clone,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328660,
    "dDurationMs": 7110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for example, if there's a repository that is not \non your local machine, but it's on GitHub, and you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335770,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "want to bring it down on your local machine, so \nyou can use it locally, you use the clone command.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341050,
    "dDurationMs": 6030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the way, all of these commands are lowercase, \nnot uppercase, as we're going to see, as we go  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347080,
    "dDurationMs": 6330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through the examples in this video, when you have \nupdated files, or created or deleted files and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353410,
    "dDurationMs": 5850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "folders, you're going to want to tell get that you \nmade changes and that you would like Git to track  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359260,
    "dDurationMs": 6450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those changes. So you use the Add command for \nthat. As I said earlier, Git is there for you to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365710,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "save the changes you make in your code. So you do \nthat through a commit, and we call it committing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372910,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your changes. Once you have made changes locally \non your computer, and you're ready to put them in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379270,
    "dDurationMs": 5910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "git, you tell Git to track them through the Add \ncommand, you save your files to get through the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385180,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "commit command. And then you upload your files \nto a place like GitHub, or another what we call  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392140,
    "dDurationMs": 6090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "remote repository, or GitHub alternative, like \nBitbucket, get lab, and there's many of them,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398230,
    "dDurationMs": 7650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you do this through the push command. When there \nare changes to your code on GitHub, and you want  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 405880,
    "dDurationMs": 6630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to bring those to your local machine, then you use \nthe pull command, you pull down changes from the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412510,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "remote repository. Those are all the commands \nthat I want to cover. To get started. Again,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417490,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we're going to go over examples of each of these. \nThe first thing you will need to do is of course,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423790,
    "dDurationMs": 5670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sign up for an account on GitHub. This is pretty \nstraightforward. You enter minimal information,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429460,
    "dDurationMs": 5430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and then you sign up for an account, you will get \nan email that will ask you to verify and then you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434890,
    "dDurationMs": 5670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can log into your account. When you log in, you \nwill either be on this page, which is your profile  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 440560,
    "dDurationMs": 7890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "page that you can access from this drop down menu, \nor you might be on the dashboard page here. Either  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448450,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way, you will have access to this green button \nhere, or this plus sign and the drop down here  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454810,
    "dDurationMs": 7530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where you can create a new repository. Now a \nrepository is basically a project. It's all of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462340,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your coding files and folders for whatever kind \nof application that you're building. For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468400,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this is a repository that I have, it's for \na curriculum app that I've been building,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473590,
    "dDurationMs": 7140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you can see the mockups pretty straightforward \napplication. And in this repository, this is my  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480730,
    "dDurationMs": 5910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "complete project with the folders and all of my \ncoding files are inside of these folders. For  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486640,
    "dDurationMs": 6090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this one application, each application or project \nis in a separate repository. So I'm going to go  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492730,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ahead and create a new repository here, give it a \nname demo repo. By the way repo is just short for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500410,
    "dDurationMs": 10650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "repository. I'm going to leave these settings as \nthey are and click Create repository. Now you can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511060,
    "dDurationMs": 6510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "create your files and folders for this repository \nlocally on your machine. Or you can create them  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517570,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "straight in the online editor on the GitHub \nwebsite. So I'm going to go ahead and create a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523810,
    "dDurationMs": 6870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "basic markdown file called a readme. And this is \nthe most basic file that you will find in almost  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530680,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every project that contains text to describe \nwhat the project is about what it does, and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 536140,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "any other relevant information. So I'm going to \nclick here, create a new file, I'm going to name  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541780,
    "dDurationMs": 9090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it readme.md for markdown. markdown is basically \nan easy way to format your text in these sort of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550870,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "files. So I'm going to come down here and type \nsome text. Now markdown has all the shortcuts  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 557350,
    "dDurationMs": 7470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like one hash tag for a main header. So I'm just \ngoing to put demo here and then some plain text,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564820,
    "dDurationMs": 8250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so I'll put some description. And then I'll scroll \nto the bottom to commit the file. In other words,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 573070,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "save this file. I just want to point out really \nquickly that this create readme.md is placeholder  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580150,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "text. But if you don't write anything else here, \nand when I commit this, it will use this text as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585730,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the default. So let me commit. And you can see \nhere that this is the commit message from when I  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593290,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "saved this README file. And now you can see the \nreadme file, which because it's named README,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599890,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dot MD is the default file that GitHub always \nshows down here. I can also go into the file here,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608050,
    "dDurationMs": 5490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is the same thing, except with a few \ndifferent options here. And in either screen,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613540,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I have this edit this file option. So I'm going to \ngo ahead and do that. I'm going to simply change  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619900,
    "dDurationMs": 8790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this to an exclamation point. Now the default \ntext instead of create readme.md is now update  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628690,
    "dDurationMs": 7230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "readme.md, which isn't that descriptive, but it's \nokay for right now. So I'm going to commit, and by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635920,
    "dDurationMs": 6870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "committing, I saved my changes here. And if I go \nto the demo repo, it shows my last commit message  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 642790,
    "dDurationMs": 7350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "here as update readme.md. Now, if I want to see \nmy entire history of changes, in other words,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 650140,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every commit that I've made, I can come here to \ncommits. And each commit has a unique identifier.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 656440,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And I can also see the message or the title that \nI wrote on these commits, I can come back to this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663160,
    "dDurationMs": 6630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one and see what was added this green with the \nplus signs means these lines were added to this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669790,
    "dDurationMs": 7740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "file. And then I'll go back, and in the updated \ncommit, it shows me this red with a minus sign,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677530,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that means this line was deleted, and then the \ngreen with the plus means this line was added,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 683050,
    "dDurationMs": 6690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "anything that's white means it stayed the same. \nSo this is a very basic view of seeing changes.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689740,
    "dDurationMs": 7770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now what about using this on your local machine, \nif you are using a Mac or Linux operating system,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 697510,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you should already have Git installed. You can \ncheck this by opening your terminal or command  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 704350,
    "dDurationMs": 9300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "line application and typing in get space dash \ndash version. If Git is not already installed,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713650,
    "dDurationMs": 6210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I highly recommend checking out this tutorial by \nAtlassian, which I will link in the description  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719860,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "below. It walks you through how to install Git \non any operating system. Now I do want to note  "
